# Skill Authoring Principles

This is a skills repository. Every directory under `skills/` is a skill. Follow [agentskills-best-practices.md](../docs/agentskills-best-practices.md) as the canonical standard.

## Context budget

Skills use progressive disclosure ([spec](https://agentskills.io/specification#progressive-disclosure)):

1. **`name` + `description`** — loaded for all skills at startup (~100 tokens each)
2. **`SKILL.md` body** — loaded on activation (< 500 lines / ~5,000 tokens)
3. **`references/`, `scripts/`, `assets/`** — loaded on demand

Every token must earn its place. Ask: *"Would the agent get this wrong without this instruction?"* Cut boilerplate the agent already knows.

## Ground in real expertise

- Extract from hands-on tasks: steps that worked, corrections you made, I/O formats, non-inferable facts.
- Synthesize from project artifacts: specs, runbooks, review patterns, fix PRs.
- Refine with real execution: read traces; add corrections to gotchas.

## Calibrate to agent knowledge

- **SolvaPay APIs/SDKs:** teach with implementation code and bundled references — the agent cannot infer our patterns.
- **Well-known tools** (Next.js, Tailwind, Supabase): behavioral direction only, not tutorials.

## Calibrating control

Match specificity to fragility:

- **High freedom** — multiple valid approaches; explain *why*
- **Medium freedom** — preferred pattern with template or pseudocode
- **Low freedom** — exact scripts/commands for fragile or destructive ops

**Defaults, not menus.** One recommended approach; alternatives only when context genuinely differs.

## Consistent terminology

Pick one term per concept within a skill. Use forward slashes in paths: `scripts/helper.py`.

## No time-sensitive information

Never date-gate instructions. Use an "Old patterns" section with `<details>` for deprecated approaches.
